WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.172 --> 00:00:04.537
- [Voiceover] This is the
Southern Appalachian Salamander.

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.206
It breathes entirely through its skin,

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:08.766
which makes it incredibly sensitive

00:00:08.766 --> 00:00:11.554
to water quality and temperature.

00:00:11.554 --> 00:00:14.134
And with average temperatures
increasing yearly,

00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:17.137
their future has become quite murky.

00:00:17.137 --> 00:00:20.412
(quiet chime music)

00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:22.055
The Appalachian region is home to

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:25.899
more salamander species than
anywhere else in the world.

00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:28.607
Adults top out at seven inches, and females

00:00:28.607 --> 00:00:31.277
are slightly larger than males.

00:00:31.277 --> 00:00:35.597
They are found across the
Carolinas, Georgia and Tennessee.

00:00:35.597 --> 00:00:37.564
They live in wet, forested areas

00:00:37.564 --> 00:00:41.725
as they need to stay moist
to respirate properly.

00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:43.559
Unfortunately, these areas have been

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:46.319
shrinking and warming up quickly.

00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:48.799
Climate change and habitat loss

00:00:48.799 --> 00:00:51.839
are grave concerns for
this remarkable species.

00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:54.690
This is the Southern
Appalachian Salamander.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.690
(quiet chime music)

